Black River Gorges was proclaimed in 1994 and is the largest national park in Mauritius. It covers about 67 km² of humid upland forest and drier lowland forest and marshy heathland. Its ravines run down from the central plateau towards the west coast at Black River.

The park is the last refuge of much of the island's native wildlife. It is home to all of its surviving endemic bird species. Among them are the Mauritius kestrel and the pink pigeon and the echo parakeet which were brought back from the edge of extinction.

Some 60 km of trails cross the park from short walks to long descents into the gorges. They start both near the coast at Black River and up on the plateau around Plaine Champagne and the Gorges Viewpoint. The highest summit in Mauritius rises above the park: the Piton de la Petite Rivière Noire (828 m).

## Good to know

- Set off early and carry water and wear proper shoes. Some trails are steep and slippery after rain.
- Tell someone your route before a long hike and keep to the marked paths.
